    "body": "\n\n## Overview\n\nThe Rust frontend creates one Tokio output channel for every active\nrequest. Each channel currently stores 304-byte output deliveries inline,\ncausing a drained but warmed channel to retain about 22.5 KB of memory.\n\nStore queued deliveries behind `Box` pointers and unwrap them at the\nreceiver boundary. The output type, ordering, error propagation, and\nstream behavior remain unchanged.\n\n\n\n## Claims\n\n- Reduces the Tokio channel slot size from 304 bytes to 8 bytes on\n  64-bit platforms.\n- Reduces measured warmed-channel RSS from about 22.5 KB to 1.6 KB per\n  active request.\n- Preserves the existing output stream API and wire behavior.\n- Keeps the implementation private to the per-request output channel.\n\nThe total memory savings increase with the number of concurrently active\nrequest streams.\n\n\n\n## Validation\n```\ncargo nextest run -p vllm-engine-core-client --all-features\ncargo clippy -p vllm-engine-core-client --all-targets  --all-features --locked  -- -D warnings\ncargo fmt --all -- --check\n```\n\nA release-mode microbenchmark repeatedly sent and drained outputs across\nindependent channels. Each scenario was run three times in a separate\nprocess, and the median result is reported below.\n\n| Queue item | RSS per channel | Change |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| Inline Result<Delivery> | 22,523.1 bytes | Baseline |\n| Boxed Result<Delivery> | 1,572.9 bytes | -93.0% |\n\n\n\n## Details\n\nTokio's unbounded MPSC channel stores messages in blocks containing 32\nslots. With a 304-byte delivery, each block reserves space for 32 complete\ndelivery values. Warming the channel can leave reusable blocks allocated\neven after all queued messages have been consumed.\n\nBoxing the queued value makes each channel slot pointer-sized. A private\nreceiver wrapper delegates to Tokio's existing poll_recv implementation\nand immediately unwraps the value, so downstream consumers continue to\nreceive Result<EngineCoreStreamDelivery>.\n\nBoxing adds one heap allocation per queued delivery in exchange for\nsubstantially smaller channel blocks. The change is limited to internal\nqueue storage and does not alter externally observable behavior.\n\n\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary> Pull Request Checklist </summary>\n\n- [ ] I used vLLM's `/pr-checklist` skill. (Mandatory for agents, optional for humans).\n- [ ] AI assistance was used during the creation of this PR.\n\n- [ ] **Design Fit:** Minimizes impact on core components, reuses existing functionality, and justifies added complexity.\n- [ ] **Testing and Validation:** Validates the change and ensures any added tests are meaningful and reliable, with CI coverage or documented CI resource constraints and validation performed outside CI.\n- [ ] **Code Quality and Style:** Keeps code and comments clear and concise, and updates relevant documentation and examples.\n- [ ] **Pull Request Contents:** Includes a brief summary and relevant links, supports claims with evidence, explains root causes and implementation trade-offs, and follows the contributing guide.\n</details>\n\n",
